{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgFT6yEfrPX3Gfay2Njqg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011aqNMBd5fH487nC6jF5w5K","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/testpwn/huntress-2026; python3 - <<'EOF'\np='reference/techniques/warmups.md'\ns=open(p).read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(a)==1,(s.count(a),a[:60])\n    s=s.replace(a,b)\nrep(\"# warmups — technique log (days 1-20; updated after every day)\",\"# warmups — technique log (days 1-21; updated after every day)\")\nrep(\"\"\"  Entrance\" (D20) -> hex of UTF-16BE + `feff` BOM. D1-D15 one layer; D16-D17 and D19-D20 two layers, the pun naming BOTH\n  (every number/word in a prompt may be a layer or a key).\"\"\",\"\"\"  Entrance\" (D20) -> hex of UTF-16BE + `feff` BOM, \"A familiar wrapper. Something compressed inside.\"/\"Under Pressure\"\n  (D21) -> GZIP inside BASE64 (`H4sI...`). D1-D15 one layer; D16-D17 and D19-D21 two layers, the pun naming BOTH\n  (every number/word in a prompt may be a layer or a key; \"familiar\" = an already-seen codec as the outer layer).\"\"\")\nrep(\"\"\"  Families: D12-D15 free-text letter maps, D16-D17 carriers over hex, D18 transposition, D19-D20 byte representation\n  under hex. Hex was a layer on 4 of the last 5 days: read endianness/width words, and read \"Same ...\" as \"yesterday's\n  codec, one knob turned\".\"\"\",\"\"\"  Families: D12-D15 free-text letter maps, D16-D17 carriers over hex, D18 transposition, D19-D20 byte representation\n  under hex, D21 COMPRESSION under base64 (new family; expect \"same wrapper, other squeeze\" next: zlib/raw deflate/bz2/\n  xz, or the same gzip under hex/base32). Read \"Same ...\" as \"yesterday's codec, one knob turned\".\"\"\")\nrep(\"\"\"Caesar+7, ROT47, Morse-over-hex, Bacon-over-hex, rail-fence-3, UTF-16LE+BOM hex, UTF-16BE+BOM hex, in that order so far).\"\"\",\n\"\"\"Caesar+7, ROT47, Morse-over-hex, Bacon-over-hex, rail-fence-3, UTF-16LE+BOM hex, UTF-16BE+BOM hex, gzip-in-base64, in that order so far; 103 x 21 on D21).\"\"\")\nrep(\"\"\"  98 x 20 in 20.4 s with solve 18.6 s -- a 7x jump for +1 line, a third of the 60 s kill budget; see Pitfalls).\"\"\",\n\"\"\"  98 x 20 in 20.4 s with solve 18.6 s). D21 `deadline: null`: TT 4.1 s end to end (solve 0.21 s); Mastery 103 x 21\n  first run KILLED at 60 s (ad-hoc solver, see Pitfalls), the carried lane solver + one branch then did 0.34 s solve.\"\"\")\n# variant axis: new compression axis after UTF axis\nrep(\"\"\"  position), UTF-32 (`fffe0000`/`0000feff`), UTF-8 BOM, another outer layer (b64/b32/binary) -- all prebuilt.\n\"\"\",\"\"\"  position), UTF-32 (`fffe0000`/`0000feff`), UTF-8 BOM, another outer layer (b64/b32/binary) -- all prebuilt.\n- **Compression layer under a text wrapper (D21)** -- values seen: single/TT = ONE standard-alphabet base64 string, padded\n  (`=` 152 B single, `==` 156 B TT), of a GZIP member; Mastery line 21 = a 36-char padded base64 fragment that is its\n  OWN complete gzip member (206/206 fragments). Every header identical: `H4sIAAAAAAACA` = `1f8b 08 00 00000000 02 03`\n  (deflate, no FNAME/FCOMMENT, mtime 0, xfl 2, OS Unix), so b64 text always starts `H4sI`; payload is plain UTF-8, no\n  inner layer, no zlib/bz2/xz/url-safe/unpadded seen. Compressed bytes are NOT printable, so any b64 branch gated on a\n  printable result rejects the line and the rot-N catch-all then returns garbage. Parse rule (D21 solver `_b64z`): strip\n  whitespace/quotes; `[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]+={0,2}`, len%4 != 1 -> re-pad, urlsafe-decode (accepts both alphabets) -> sniff\n  magic (`1f8b` gzip, `78` zlib, `BZh` bz2, `fd377a585a` xz) -> decompress -> UTF-8. Run it on the WHOLE blob BEFORE the\n  numbered-line check and register it as a free codec (`decode.register_free(\"b64z\", 21, ...)`) for the Mastery line.\n  Generalise next to: any wrapper (hex/b32/b85/binary) x any compressor (raw deflate `zlib.decompress(b,-15)`, zlib with\n  any level byte `7801/5e/9c/da`, bz2, xz, lzma-alone `5d00`, zstd `28b52ffd`, lz4 `04224d18`), then re-run the full\n  decoder on the output (the inflated text may itself be another day's codec).\n\"\"\")\nrep(\"\"\"| Magic `1f8b`/`78`/`BZh`/`fd377a58` | gunzip / zlib / bzip2 / xz (in the toolbox. Not seen in the GM bytes yet) |\"\"\",\n\"\"\"| base64 starting `H4sI` (D21; prompt \"wrapper\", \"compressed\", \"pressure\", \"squeeze\", \"inflate\"); also `eJ`/`eNo` zlib, `QlpoOTFBWSZT` bz2, `/Td6WFo` xz, `KLUv/` zstd, `BCJNGA` lz4, `UEsDB` zip. Hex: `1f8b`, `78xx`, `425a68`, `fd377a585a`, `28b52ffd`, `504b0304` | `base64 -d blob.txt \\| gzip -dc` (D21, one command); python `gzip.decompress(base64.b64decode(t))`; zlib `zlib.decompress`, headerless `zlib.decompress(b,-15)`; `lib.decode.peel` does the stdlib ones. Triage says \"looks base64\" -- right, but the bytes are binary: decompress, don't score |\"\"\")\n# day by day: compress D20 entry into D14-20 paragraph and add D21\nold_start=s.index(\"**Days 14-19**\")\nold_end=s.index(\"## GM answer conventions learned\")\nnew_days=\"\"\"**Days 14-20** (Caesar +7; ROT47; Morse over hex; Bacon a/b 5-groups over hex; rail fence 3/offset 0; hex of UTF-16LE\n+ `fffe` BOM; hex of UTF-16BE + `feff` BOM): CORRECT in 4.5-39 s, first submit each day. Lessons that still matter: D14\nset22 went MISSING when a rev/Atbash swap ended in a 2-word slug (rank 3-word slugs first). D15 ROT47 is beyond\nrot-N/Atbash/reversal -> `register_free`. D16/D17 a carrier's 16-symbol output is a middle layer (`fromhex`). D17\nMastery (83 x 17) hit the 60 s harness budget until `answer()` returned early. D18 the anagram was cracked by rail r\n2..N x every offset (wrong offsets give ROTATED copies; accept only reads starting `Nice work`); lib's `rail_solve`\nexisted but the carried solver never routed to it; a TT start before a verified solver burned the 30 s clock. D19 one\ncommand (`xxd -r -p | iconv -f UTF-16 -t UTF-8`), ~26 s; the duel worker's BOM-aware `_hex_utf16` (LE/B